   "name": "cool-compress-fever.md",
   "text": "title: Cool compress (for fever and insect bites)\ntags: health remedy\nwarn: Do not apply ice or an ice-cold compress directly to bare skin for extended periods — wrap ice in cloth and limit applications to 10–15 minutes to avoid frostbite or tissue damage. For fever, cool compresses supplement (but do not replace) appropriate fever-reducing medicine when indicated; consult a healthcare provider if fever is high, prolonged, or accompanied by severe symptoms.\nsource: https://medlineplus.gov/insectbitesandstings.html, as summarised by Project NOMAD (home_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/home_remedies.json#cool-compress-fever\n\nA clean cloth soaked in cool (not ice-cold) water and placed on the forehead or bitten area can help reduce discomfort from fever, insect bites, and eye allergy symptoms. For insect stings, ice wrapped in a washcloth should be applied for 10 minutes on and 10 minutes off.\n\n## how\nSoak a clean cloth in cool water, wring it out, and place it on the forehead or affected area. For insect stings, wrap ice in a cloth and apply for 10 minutes on, then 10 minutes off — never place ice directly on bare skin.\n\n## evidence\nMedlinePlus (NIH/NIAID) notes that applying cool compresses is recommended for allergic conjunctivitis and eye burning and irritation. For insect bites and stings, a cool or iced compress is a standard first-line self-care step described in MedlinePlus search guidance consistent with NIH resources.\n\n## safety\nDo not apply ice or an ice-cold compress directly to bare skin for extended periods — wrap ice in cloth and limit applications to 10–15 minutes to avoid frostbite or tissue damage. For fever, cool compresses supplement (but do not replace) appropriate fever-reducing medicine when indicated; consult a healthcare provider if fever is high, prolonged, or accompanied by severe symptoms.\n\n## used for\n- Fever\n- Insect bites & stings\n- Eye allergies\n- Eye irritation & dryness\n\n## also said\n- my kid is burning up with fever\n- how to bring a fever down without medicine\n- forehead very hot, nothing to give\n- wet cloth on forehead for fever does it help\n- stung by a bee and it hurts\n- wasp sting swelling what to do\n- bug bite itching and swollen\n- mosquito bites everywhere on my child\n- how to cool down someone with a high temperature\n- eyes itchy and watery from pollen\n- eyes burning and irritated\n- allergy eyes red puffy no drops\n- how long to put ice on a sting\n- cool compress how to make\n- got bitten by something, red bump, what now\n- elderly father has fever, how to keep him cool\n- temperature 39 and no paracetamol\n- baby feels hot, how to cool safely\n- ant bites hurt so bad\n- fever wont go down when to worry\n- my eye is dry and stinging\n- fiever child, cold water on head ok?\n"
