   "name": "oral-rehydration.md",
   "text": "title: Oral rehydration solution (ORS)\naka: ORS, rehydration salts\ntags: health remedy\nwarn: Seek immediate medical care for signs of severe dehydration: no urination for 8 or more hours, rapid heartbeat, confusion, or inability to keep fluids down. Infants and small children with diarrhea should use formulated ORS (not plain water) to replace electrolytes safely.\nsource: https://medlineplus.gov/dehydration.html, as summarised by Project NOMAD (home_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/home_remedies.json#oral-rehydration\n\nOral rehydration solutions replace fluids and electrolytes lost through diarrhea or other causes of dehydration. For mild to moderate dehydration, drinking water is the first step; sports drinks or oral rehydration solutions (such as Pedialyte) are recommended when electrolytes are also depleted, especially for children.\n\n## how\nUse a commercially prepared oral rehydration solution (available without a prescription) and follow the package directions. For adults with electrolyte losses, sports drinks can help; if liquids are hard to keep down, take small sips frequently or suck on ice chips rather than drinking large amounts at once.\n\n## evidence\nMedlinePlus (NIH/NIDDK) states that treatment for dehydration involves replacing lost fluids and electrolytes, and that oral rehydration solutions for children are available without a prescription. The same source recommends sports drinks for adults when electrolytes have been lost alongside fluids.\n\n## safety\nSeek immediate medical care for signs of severe dehydration: no urination for 8 or more hours, rapid heartbeat, confusion, or inability to keep fluids down. Infants and small children with diarrhea should use formulated ORS (not plain water) to replace electrolytes safely.\n\n## used for\n- Dehydration\n- Diarrhea\n\n## also said\n- kid has been throwing up all day and wont drink\n- baby has diarrhea, what to give\n- toddler diarrhea for two days, is water enough\n- signs of dehydration in a child\n- no pee for 8 hours is that bad\n- sunken eyes dry mouth after diarrhea\n- how to replace electrolytes\n- lost a lot of fluid from diarrhea, feel dizzy\n- what is ORS and how do i use it\n- oral rehydration solution for kids\n- is pedialyte better than water for a baby\n- grandpa very weak and confused after days of diarrhea\n- dehydrated, heart beating fast, what now\n- cant keep anything down and getting dehydrated\n- how do i know if someone is dehydrated\n- infant with diarrhea plain water ok?\n- rehydration salts how much\n- sports drink for dehydration ok\n- my child is lethargic and not peeing much\n- severe diarrhea how to not get dried out\n- dehidrated after sick, what to drink\n- diarrhea won't stop worried about water loss\n"
