   "name": "saline-nasal-rinse.md",
   "text": "title: Saline nasal rinse\naka: neti pot, nasal irrigation, saline nasal wash\ntags: health remedy\nwarn: Use only distilled, sterile, or previously boiled (and cooled) water — never tap water. The FDA warns that tap water can harbor organisms including amoebas that are safe to swallow but can cause serious or potentially fatal infections in the nasal passages.\nsource: https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/rinsing-your-sinuses-neti-pots-safe, as summarised by Project NOMAD (home_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/home_remedies.json#saline-nasal-rinse\n\nSaline nasal rinses flush pollen, dust, and excess mucus from the nasal passages and add moisture. They can be performed with a neti pot, squeeze bottle, or bulb syringe using a prepared saline solution.\n\n## how\nUse only distilled, sterile, or previously boiled-and-cooled water — never tap water. After each use, rinse the device with the same safe water, then air-dry it thoroughly or wipe dry before storing.\n\n## evidence\nCDC recommends saline nasal spray or drops as a home care measure for the common cold. The FDA confirms that nasal irrigation devices are 'usually safe and effective products when used and cleaned properly,' with the critical safety requirement being the type of water used.\n\n## safety\nUse only distilled, sterile, or previously boiled (and cooled) water — never tap water. The FDA warns that tap water can harbor organisms including amoebas that are safe to swallow but can cause serious or potentially fatal infections in the nasal passages. Boiled water should be cooled to lukewarm and stored in a clean, closed container for no more than 24 hours. Always clean and dry the device after each use.\n\n## used for\n- Nasal congestion\n- Common cold\n\n## also said\n- how to use a neti pot\n- rinsing my nose with salt water\n- can i use tap water in a neti pot\n- what water for nasal rinse\n- sinus full of pollen, want to flush it\n- nose full of dust after working outside\n- nasal irrigation how to do\n- salt water nose spray homemade\n- how long can boiled water be kept for nose rinse\n- how to clean the nasal rinse bottle\n- saline drops for a baby's blocked nose\n- flush out my sinuses\n- is it dangerous to put tap water in my nose\n- amoeba from water in nose\n- wash nose with salt water for cold\n- squeeze bottle nose wash\n- bulb syringe nose for kid\n- hay fever nose clogged, want to rinse\n- only river water, can i rinse my nose with it\n- nose rinse with boiled water how cool\n- put salt water in nose safe?\n- sinus feel blocked, rinse is ok?\n"
